[6.459243, "o", "\u001b[36m\u001b[1m> Bash\u001b[0m cat README.md job.py fetch.py parse.py validate.py inventory.py; echo ----; cat data/count.csv data/cron.log\r\n    data/orders.csv\r\n"]
[6.49002, "o", "\u001b[2m  # order-sync\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[2m  \u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[2m  A scheduled job that takes the new orders from the sales system and applies them to the warehouse inventory: for \u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[2m  every order, the ordered quantity leaves the stock of that SKU.\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[2m  \u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[2m  It uses only the Python standard library (Python 3.12 or newer). The folder is self-contained: copy it anywhere and \u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[2m  it runs.\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[2m  \u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[2m  ## Run it\u001b[0m\r\n"]
[6.490176, "o", "\u001b[2m  \u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[2m  ```bash\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[2m  ./reset.sh            # rebuild data/orders.csv and data/inventory.db from seed/\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[2m  ... 552 more lines\u001b[0m\r\n\r\n"]
[11.159192, "o", "\u001b[36m\u001b[1m> Bash\u001b[0m python3 inventory.py --count data/count.csv; for t in stock applied_orders backorders; do echo \"== $t\"; sqlite3\r\n    -header data/inventory.db \"select * from $t\" 2>/dev/null || python3 -c \"import \r\n    sqlite3;c=sqlite3.connect('data/inventory.db');[print(r) for r in c.execute('select * from $t')]\"; done\r\n"]
[11.306077, "o", "\u001b[2m  SKU        ITEM                   ON HAND COUNTED  DIFF\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[2m  CAB-0302   HDMI cable 2 m             219     219    +0\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[2m  DOC-0501   Laptop dock                  0       0    +0\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[2m  HDS-0402   Headset                     61      57    +4\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[2m  HUB-0301   USB-C hub                   61      61    +0\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[2m  KBD-0101   Mechanical keyboard        103      94    +9\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[2m  MON-0201   27-inch monitor             38      37    +1\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[2m  MSE-0102   Wireless mouse             172     172    +0\u001b[0m\r\n"]
[11.306117, "o", "\u001b[2m  WEB-0401   Webcam 1080p                51      51    +0\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[2m  == stock\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[2m  ('KBD-0101', 'Mechanical keyboard', 103, 1)\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[2m  ('MSE-0102', 'Wireless mouse', 172, 5)\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[2m  ... 43 more lines\u001b[0m\r\n\r\n"]
[20.838222, "o", "\u001b[36m\u001b[1m> Bash\u001b[0m S=/tmp/claude-1000/-tmp-morning-plain/27454cc2-b468-4691-bb39-f5680f2b0b71/scratchpad && mkdir -p $S && cp\r\n    data/inventory.db $S/inv.db && ORDER_SYNC_LOG_LEVEL=INFO python3 job.py --source api --db $S/inv.db; echo exit=$?; \r\n    python3 -c \"\r\n    import sys; sys.path.insert(0,'.'); import fetch,json\r\n    for r in fetch.from_csv('data/orders.csv'): print(json.dumps(r))\" | cat -A | head -20\r\n"]
[21.487471, "o", "\u001b[2m  2026-11-26 17:49:01,031 order_sync INFO fetching orders from http://127.0.0.1:38397\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[2m  2026-11-26 17:49:01,539 order_sync INFO 9 orders read, 8 applied in this run\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[2m  sync finished: OK\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[2m  exit=0\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[2m  {\"order_id\": \"SO-20538\", \"placed_at\": \"2026-11-25T21:30\", \"sku\": \"CAB-0302\", \"quantity\": \"4\", \"channel\": \u001b[0m\r\n"]
[21.487606, "o", "\u001b[2m  \"marketplace\", \"unit\": \"each\"}$\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[2m  {\"order_id\": \"SO-20539\", \"placed_at\": \"2026-11-26T06:10\", \"sku\": \"MSE-0102\", \"quantity\": \"2\", \"channel\": \"web\", \u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[2m  \"unit\": \"each\"}$\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[2m  {\"order_id\": \"SO-20540\", \"placed_at\": \"2026-11-26T06:48\", \"sku\": \"KBD-0101\", \"quantity\": \"3\", \"channel\": \u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[2m  \"marketplace\", \"unit\": \"each\"}$\u001b[0m\r\n"]
[21.487661, "o", "\u001b[2m  {\"order_id\": \"SO-20541\", \"placed_at\": \"2026-11-26T07:15\", \"sku\": \"CAB-0302\", \"quantity\": \"1\", \"channel\": \"store\", \u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[2m  \"unit\": \"pack\"}$\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[2m  ... 10 more lines\u001b[0m\r\n\r\n"]
[30.869264, "o", "\u001b[36m\u001b[1m> Bash\u001b[0m S=/tmp/claude-1000/-tmp-morning-plain/27454cc2-b468-4691-bb39-f5680f2b0b71/scratchpad && cp data/inventory.db\r\n    $S/inv2.db && printf 'order_id,placed_at,sku,quantity,channel,unit\\nT-1,2026-11-26T10:00,KBD-0101 \r\n    ,3,marketplace,each\\nT-2,2026-11-26T10:01,hds-0402,2,web,each\\nT-3,2026-11-26T10:02,MON-0201,1,web,unit\\n' > \r\n    $S/t.csv && python3 job.py --orders $S/t.csv --db $S/inv2.db; echo exit=$?; echo \"--- with logging:\"; \r\n    ORDER_SYNC_LOG_LEVEL=WARNING python3 job.py --orders $S/t.csv --db $S/inv2.db\r\n"]
[31.086376, "o", "\u001b[2m  sync finished: OK\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[2m  exit=0\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[2m  --- with logging:\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[2m  2026-11-26 17:49:11,184 order_sync WARNING order T-1 skipped: SKU 'KBD-0101 ' is not in the format AAA-0000\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[2m  2026-11-26 17:49:11,184 order_sync WARNING order T-2 skipped: SKU 'hds-0402' is not in the format AAA-0000\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[2m  2026-11-26 17:49:11,184 order_sync WARNING order T-3 skipped: unit 'unit' is not one of each, pack\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[2m  sync finished: OK\u001b[0m\r\n\r\n"]
[42.177741, "o", "The run said OK because the job reports OK even when it skips orders, and it skips them without telling anyone. Last \r\nnight five orders were dropped this way, and that accounts for the wrong stock.\r\n\r\n**What's wrong with the stock** (`python3 inventory.py --count data/count.csv`):\r\n\r\n| SKU | On hand | Counted | Diff |\r\n|---|---|---|---|\r\n| KBD-0101 | 103 | 94 | +9 |\r\n| HDS-0402 | 61 | 57 | +4 |\r\n| MON-0201 | 38 | 37 | +1 |\r\n\r\nAll other SKUs match the count. `applied_orders` holds SO-20501 to SO-20538 except **SO-20521, SO-20526, SO-20530, \r\nSO-20533 and SO-20537**. Those five orders were never taken out of stock, which explains the 14 extra units.\r\n\r\n**Why the run still said OK** (`job.py`, `sync_orders`):\r\n```python\r\nexcept Exception as exc:\r\n    log.warning(\"order %s skipped: %s\", order.order_id, exc)\r\n    continue\r\n```\r\n- If an order fails validation or names an unknown SKU, it is logged at WARNING and skipped.\r\n- The default log level is ERROR (`ORDER_SYNC_LOG_LEVEL`), so that warning is never printed.\r\n- `main()` always prints `sync finished: OK` and exits 0, however many orders were skipped.\r\n- Cron's output is the only record of a run, so nothing shows the skips.\r\n\r\nI confirmed this on a copy of the database in the scratchpad (the real one wasn't touched). Bad sample orders produced \r\nonly `sync finished: OK`, exit 0. With `ORDER_SYNC_LOG_LEVEL=WARNING` each one printed \"skipped\".\r\n\r\n**Why it's permanent:** the sales API hands each order over only once. The five skipped orders aren't in the current \r\nfeed, so rerunning the job won't bring them back. They'll have to be fetched from the sales system some other way, or \r\nthe stock corrected by hand from the count. I also ran the current feed (SO-20538 to SO-20546) against a copy, and it \r\ngoes through without skips.\r\n\r\n**What I can't tell you:** why those five orders failed. Their data is gone and no log was kept. My strongest guess is \r\nthe SKU field. `parse.py` strips spaces from and normalises `order_id`, `channel` and `unit`, but leaves `sku` \r\nuntouched, so `\"KBD-0101 \"` or `\"kbd-0101\"` fails the `AAA-0000` check. Other possible causes are a unit other than \r\n`each`/`pack`, a quantity outside 1–500, or a SKU that isn't in stock.\r\n\r\n**Suggested fixes:**\r\n1. Make `sync_orders` report skipped orders. Print them, or log them at ERROR, and exit non-zero (or at least not print\r\n OK) when any are skipped, so cron mails you.\r\n2. Strip spaces from SKUs and uppercase them in `parse.py`.\r\n3. Keep skipped orders somewhere, such as a table or file, so they can be fixed and applied later instead of lost.\r\n\r\nWant me to make these changes?\r\n\r\n"]
[45.54691, "o", "\u001b[2m-- end of the run: 5 tool calls, 45 s --\u001b[0m\r\n"]
